  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nLight shower activity continues in the mountains tonight as \nwesterly flow persists. The increasing cloud cover and rising \ndewpoints are going to make for a comparatively mild night with \ntemperatures only falling into the low to mid-40s. \n\nShower activity increases again beginning Tuesday morning as a \nweak cold front sinks southward into our area. The trend has \nbeen for the front and its accompanying precipitation to only \nmake it as far south as the foothills with southern Maine and \nsouthern New Hampshire standing the best chance of remaining \ndry. This activity starts widely scattered and light, but a more\nsurprising trend has been an increase in QPF Tuesday night as a\nwarm air advection regime takes hold and the front begins to \nlift back north. The 02.12Z REFS mean suggests the mountains \ncould end up seeing 0.25 to 0.50 inches with isolated higher \namounts in central Somerset County. The foothills may see a \ntenth to a quarter of an inch, with a few hundredths possible \ndown to a line extending from Lebanon, New Hampshire to \nPortland, Maine. \n  \nShower activity comes to an end Wednesday morning as the front \npushes back out of our area. Skies clear as well and allow for \nour warming trend to peak as temperatures are able to mix into \nthe low to mid 70s across much of the area. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nLatest NWP model guidance continues to indicate that a frontal\nboundary will arrive from the west mid-week before stalling near\nNew England late Thursday through at least Friday. During this\ntime period, multiple weak waves of low pressure are progged to\nride along this boundary, bringing our best potential for a\nwidespread soaking rainfall in several weeks. That being said,\nthere is significant uncertainty in where this frontal boundary\nwill ultimately set-up, which will play a vital role in\ndetermining where the heaviest rainfall occurs. \n\nSome model runs and ensemble suites keep the main focus of\nrainfall offshore and/or east of our area while other guidance\nbrings widespread soaking rainfall to the entire region. Latest\nensemble probabilities for >=1\" are low to moderate, with the\nhighest probabilities across ME but the probabilities for >=2\"\nare significantly lower. There are also large timing\ndifferences, with some guidance showing showers beginning late\nThursday and rain persisting through Saturday while other\nguidance brings the onset of rainfall later into Friday. The\nmain takeaway is that while a beneficial soaking is possible,\ngiven the trend over the last several weeks, guidance may trend\nlower on rainfall as we get closer in time. \n\nTemperatures will remain above avg through late week before\ntrending closer to avg this weekend into next week.",
